WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.069
Mix sand and stones together
in a bowl. That is a mixture.

2
00:00:08.289 --> 00:00:12.515
Two materials in one place, and
each one stays exactly itself.

3
00:00:13.015 --> 00:00:16.405
Here is sand. Fine grains that
pour through your fingers.

4
00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:20.526
Here are stones. Big and
hard. They do not pour at all.

5
00:00:20.746 --> 00:00:24.740
Mix them in a bowl. That is a
mixture: two materials together.

6
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.232
You might think that mixing makes
one new material. It does not.

7
00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:34.189
Look closely. The sand is still
sand, the stones still stones.

8
00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:37.660
Nothing changed. Each material
keeps its own properties.

9
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.968
Each material kept its properties,
so you can get them apart.

10
00:00:42.188 --> 00:00:46.460
Sand and stones are very
different sizes. So use a sieve.

11
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.628
Shake it. The fine sand falls
straight through the holes.

12
00:00:50.848 --> 00:00:54.934
The stones are too big and
stay behind. Separated by size.

13
00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:59.289
Iron filings and sand are both
tiny. A sieve cannot help.

14
00:00:59.509 --> 00:01:03.085
But only the iron is magnetic.
Sweep a magnet over them.

15
00:01:03.305 --> 00:01:07.113
The filings jump onto the
magnet. The sand is left behind.

16
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:12.906
Careful: not every metal is magnetic.
Iron and steel are; copper wire is not.

17
00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:17.214
Sand in water is a mixture too.
Both of them are still there.

18
00:01:17.434 --> 00:01:22.867
Put a funnel over a jug. Line it
with filter paper. Pour it slowly.

19
00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:26.710
The water drips through into
the jug below, and it is clear.

20
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.691
The sand cannot get through the
tiny holes. It stays behind.

21
00:01:30.911 --> 00:01:35.091
A funnel only guides the liquid.
The paper does the separating.

22
00:01:35.591 --> 00:01:39.399
Now stir a spoon of salt into
water. Watch it disappear.

23
00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:43.752
Has the salt gone for ever?
No. It is still there.

24
00:01:43.972 --> 00:01:47.548
Taste one drop, with a
grown-up. The water is salty.

25
00:01:47.768 --> 00:01:51.622
So the salt is still in the
glass, in pieces too small to see.

26
00:01:51.842 --> 00:01:55.743
Dissolving is a kind of
mixing. It is not a vanishing.

27
00:01:55.963 --> 00:02:00.143
Salt and sugar dissolve.
Sand and pebbles do not.

28
00:02:00.643 --> 00:02:05.984
Practical work has risks. A risk is
something that could hurt someone.

29
00:02:06.204 --> 00:02:09.733
Glass can break. Floors get
wet. Some powders can hurt you.

30
00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:14.643
A scientist spots the risks
first: goggles on, spills wiped.

31
00:02:14.863 --> 00:02:20.065
Hair tied back. And taste nothing
unless a grown-up says it is safe.

32
00:02:20.565 --> 00:02:24.559
A mixture holds two materials,
and each keeps its properties.

33
00:02:24.779 --> 00:02:29.748
A sieve separates by size. A magnet
pulls the iron out of the sand.

34
00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:33.497
A filter holds the sand back
and lets the water through.

35
00:02:33.717 --> 00:02:38.222
Dissolved salt is still there.
And a scientist spots the risks.
